    domain: 'Craft and Structure',
    skill: 'Words in Context',
    difficulty: 'hard',
    stimulus: `Political theorists have long grappled with the tension between procedural and substantive conceptions of justice. Procedural accounts hold that an outcome is just if it results from a fair process, regardless of what that outcome happens to be. Substantive accounts, by contrast, evaluate outcomes directly against independent criteria of fairness or welfare, without regard to the process that generated them. A persistent challenge for procedural theorists is the possibility that an ostensibly fair procedure can yield outcomes that are, by almost any independent reckoning, deeply unjust — raising the question of whether procedural legitimacy alone can do the moral work the theory assigns to it.`,
